The Pacific Ocean is running more than 2°C hotter than normal, and Japan’s weather agency says its measurements are the highest it has ever recorded. NOAA gives this El Niño an 83% chance of reaching historic strength by the end of the year, and the peak hasn’t even arrived yet.

In this video we cover:

what El Niño actually is (trade winds, the warm pool and why the rain moves);
why scientists say this one is different;
the deadly 1877 El Niño and the 1997 “climate event of the century”;
what the Bureau of Meteorology outlook means for Australia: drier in the south-east, bushfire risk in 7 states and territories, Western Australia’s twist and Perth’s water storage;
the Great Barrier Reef and coral bleaching;
floods, drought and food prices around the world (cocoa, coffee, rice, palm oil);
why 2027 could break global temperature records;
what comes after: La Niña.

Every El Niño is different. Tell us in the comments: what’s the weather doing where you live right now?

Subscribe to Factuality for science, space, oceans and weather explained.

